I am trying to build nethack 3.1 on a Sparc classic running Solaris
2.1 with the unbundled SUN C compiler.

I succeeded with the tty interface and it seems to run fine. I am
working on the X interface which the notes say haven't been tested. I
found and worked around a couple of SUN bugs in the build and I'm
still having trouble getting it to run. I hope the dev team gets this
and looks into it. If anyone else is trying this build let me know
your problems/answers.

The first SUN bug is with the library libXmu.a. The normal load uses
the dynamic libraries and I was getting two missing references to
routines with the string 'ShellWidgetClass" (I am not at the SUN now
so I don't have the full names). I located the routines in libXmu.a
and wondered why they weren't loaded in when the library was on the
link command line. I tried to explicitly link the whole library in and
it worked!!  The problem is that the dynamic library doesn't have that
module!!!  Solaris strikes again!!!!

A simpler solution is to extract the missing module from the library
and link with that so you don't statically load the whole library.

The second problem was easy to solve. The load needs the libm.a
library. One of the SUN X libs refers to sin, cos etc. Just add -lm to
the WINX11LIBS macro in the makefile.


Now for the real problem:

The X11 version comes up with this error message: (close to this)
the font is not monospaced

The display looks ok but there is no dungeon and no reaction to
keystrokes.  It lets me select a character and I can quit from the
window menu and it shows me my posessions but no dungeon. I followed
the instructions for Openwindows 3.0 to make the fonts available but
it doesn't seem to work. I ran convertfont with the -F option for
fixed width conversion but it didn't help. I have a local font dir
which I reference using xset and then I do an xset fp rehash.  I can't
seem to display the nethack fonts with the font display utilities
either.

I will summarize if anyone wants to both newsgroups so email any responses.

